{
  "dom_id": "step-q61105",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217432/assets/25e1891ce31fa7043723b2dcb4cd1e01c41dbd82ae70ef5024c64ed2d6deab33.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(-3,1)$  and $(-3,-5)?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217432/assets/1f3eeede9aa301cf6d64b48cc86f770fc86517ed51285da9bdc86e5a580b243f.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217432/assets/a504ca22769a377cd7e23e0b6a09595d00c8ad25733f3d1e6a716b52f262e139.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5+1=6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
